var e={module:`web`,levels:[{title:`First Clicks`,blurb:`Meet browsers, servers, URLs, and the friendly DevTools that let you peek inside any page.`},{title:`Talking HTTP`,blurb:`Learn how browsers and servers chat with methods, status codes, headers, and DNS.`},{title:`DOM Hands-On`,blurb:`Change the page with JavaScript: pick elements, edit them, and react to user events.`},{title:`Fetch & Store`,blurb:`Call JSON APIs, save data in the browser, load scripts smartly, and use npm.`},{title:`Ship It Well`,blurb:`Build pages that are fast, accessible, and safe, the way real teams do.`}],questions:[{id:`web-004`,level:1,topic:`Client & Server`,type:`single`,prompt:`When you visit a website, what role does your browser play?`,choices:[`The client`,`The database`,`The server`,`The domain`],answer:0,hint:`One side asks for things, the other side answers.`,explain:`Your browser is the client: it sends requests. The server is the computer that receives those requests and sends back responses.`},{id:`web-018`,level:1,topic:`HTTPS`,type:`tf`,prompt:`HTTPS sends data between the browser and the server as readable plain text.`,answer:!1,hint:`Remember what the S adds.`,explain:`False. HTTPS encrypts the data, so people on the same network cannot read it. Plain HTTP is the one without encryption.`},{id:`web-003`,level:1,topic:`URLs`,type:`single`,prompt:"In `https://example.com`, what is the `https` part called?",choices:[`The file name`,`The domain extension`,`The page title`,`The protocol`],answer:3,hint:`It tells the browser which set of communication rules to use.`,explain:"The first part of a URL is the protocol, also called the scheme. `https` tells the browser to use secure HTTP to talk to the server."},{id:`web-002`,level:1,topic:`URLs`,type:`single`,prompt:`In this URL, which part is the domain name?`,code:`https://www.example.com/about`,lang:`text`,choices:["`example.com/about`","`https`","`/about`","`www.example.com`"],answer:3,hint:"The domain is the human-friendly name of the site, between `://` and the first `/`.",explain:"The domain (host name) is `www.example.com`. It names the server you want to talk to, while `/about` is the path to a page on that server."},{id:`web-013`,level:1,topic:`URLs`,type:`single`,prompt:"In this URL, what is `/blog/first-post`?",code:`https://example.com/blog/first-post`,lang:`text`,choices:[`The protocol`,`The port`,`The domain`,`The path`],answer:3,hint:`It comes after the domain and points to a place on the site.`,explain:`The path tells the server which page or resource you want on that site. Here it points to the first blog post.`},{id:`web-014`,level:1,topic:`Client & Server`,type:`single`,prompt:"You open this address while building a site. What does `localhost` mean?",code:`http://localhost:3000`,lang:`text`,choices:[`A server owned by Google`,`Your own computer`,`A website about local news`,`The nearest Wi-Fi router`],answer:1,hint:`Local means close to home.`,explain:"`localhost` always points back to the same computer you are using. Developers use it to view sites running on their own machine."},{id:`web-020`,level:1,topic:`Browser`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are web browsers? Select all that apply.`,choices:[`Chrome`,`Safari`,`Node.js`,`Firefox`],answer:[0,1,3],hint:`One of these runs JavaScript outside of any browser window.`,explain:`Chrome, Firefox, and Safari are browsers that load and show web pages. Node.js runs JavaScript on a computer or server, but it does not show web pages.`},{id:`web-022`,level:1,topic:`Page Loading`,type:`fill`,prompt:"When you visit `https://example.com/`, many servers send a default file for that folder. What is its usual file name? Type the full file name.",answer:[`index.html`,`/index.html`],hint:"It is the same file name in most beginner projects, with a `.html` ending.",explain:"Many servers send `index.html` when the URL ends with a folder like `/`. That is why the home page file is often called `index.html`."},{id:`web-005`,level:1,topic:`Client & Server`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does a web server do?`,choices:[`It runs the page's JavaScript inside your browser tab`,`It draws the web page on the visitor's screen`,`It saves a copy of every page you visit`,`It answers requests by sending back files or data`],answer:3,hint:`It sits on the other end of the request your browser sends.`,explain:`A server waits for requests and responds with things like HTML pages, images, or JSON data. Drawing the page and running its JavaScript is the browser's job.`},{id:`web-010`,level:1,topic:`Page Loading`,type:`single`,prompt:`When you open a web page, which file does the browser usually receive first?`,choices:[`The HTML document`,`The CSS stylesheets`,`The JavaScript files`,`The images`],answer:0,hint:`This file tells the browser what other files the page needs.`,explain:`The browser first gets the HTML. While reading it, the browser finds links to CSS, JavaScript, and images, and then requests those files.`},{id:`web-016`,level:1,topic:`HTTP`,type:`tf`,prompt:`HTTP stands for HyperText Transfer Protocol.`,answer:!0,hint:`HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language. HTTP shares the first word.`,explain:`Yes! HTTP is the HyperText Transfer Protocol, the set of rules browsers and servers use to exchange web pages and data.`},{id:`web-008`,level:1,topic:`HTTP`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which HTTP method does this request use?`,code:`POST /login HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com`,lang:`http`,choices:["`HOST`","`POST`","`GET`","`HTTP`"],answer:1,hint:`Look at the very first word of the first line.`,explain:"The first word of an HTTP request line is the method. Here it is `POST`, which sends data to the server, such as login details. `HTTP/1.1` at the end is the protocol version."},{id:`web-007`,level:1,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does the HTTP status code `200` mean?",choices:[`The server is too busy right now`,`The page moved`,`OK, the request worked`,`You are not logged in`],answer:2,hint:`This is the code you hope to see most of the time.`,explain:"`200 OK` is the standard success response. The server understood the request and sent back what you asked for."},{id:`web-006`,level:1,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does the HTTP status code `404` mean?",choices:[`Success`,`Server crashed`,`Not Found`,`Permission granted`],answer:2,hint:`You often see it when you mistype a page address.`,explain:"`404 Not Found` means the server could not find anything at the URL you asked for. It is very common after a typo or a deleted page."},{id:`web-019`,level:1,topic:`URLs`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are parts of the URL below? Select all that apply.`,code:`https://example.com/shop`,lang:`text`,choices:["The domain `example.com`","The protocol `https`","The path `/shop`","The file name `shop.html`"],answer:[0,1,2],hint:`Read the URL exactly as it is written. Do not add anything to it.`,explain:"This URL has a protocol, a domain, and a path. There is no `.html` file name in it: a path like `/shop` does not need a file extension."},{id:`web-023`,level:1,topic:`HTTP`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Which HTTP method does the browser send when you type a URL and press Enter? Type the method name in capital letters.`,answer:[`GET`],hint:`It is the method for reading a page, not for sending new data.`,explain:"Loading a page is a `GET` request. It asks the server to send back the resource at that URL."},{id:`web-017`,level:1,topic:`Client & Server`,type:`tf`,prompt:`A website's frontend (its HTML, CSS, and JavaScript) is loaded and shown in the visitor's browser.`,answer:!0,hint:`Where does the page actually show up and react to clicks?`,explain:`True. The frontend is the part of a site that visitors see and click. It runs on the visitor's device, while the backend runs on a server.`},{id:`web-001`,level:1,topic:`Browser`,type:`single`,prompt:`What is the main job of a web browser?`,choices:[`Store every website on your computer`,`Request web pages and display them`,`Write HTML code for you`,`Host websites for other people`],answer:1,hint:`Think about what happens when you type an address and press Enter.`,explain:`A browser such as Chrome or Firefox asks servers for pages and then draws them on your screen. It does not host or write the sites itself.`},{id:`web-015`,level:1,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does DOM stand for?`,choices:[`Document Object Model`,`Digital Online Map`,`Data Output Method`,`Display Order Manager`],answer:0,hint:`The letters name what the browser builds from your HTML, so JavaScript can work with it.`,explain:`The DOM (Document Object Model) is the browser's live, tree-shaped version of the page. JavaScript uses it to read and change what is on screen.`},{id:`web-011`,level:1,topic:`DevTools`,type:`single`,prompt:`In most desktop browsers on Windows or Linux, which key opens the developer tools?`,choices:[`F12`,`Esc`,`Tab`,`F1`],answer:0,hint:`It is one of the function keys near the top right of the keyboard.`,explain:`F12 opens DevTools in Chrome, Edge, and Firefox. On a Mac you can use Cmd+Option+I instead.`},{id:`web-012`,level:1,topic:`DevTools`,type:`single`,prompt:`Where can you see the message printed by this code?`,code:`console.log("Hello, web!")`,lang:`js`,choices:[`In a pop-up alert box`,`In the page itself, below the other content`,`In the Console panel of DevTools`,`In the browser address bar`],answer:2,hint:`The method name is a big clue.`,explain:"`console.log` writes messages to the Console panel in DevTools, not to the page. It is a great way to check what your code is doing."},{id:`web-009`,level:1,topic:`HTTPS`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does the padlock icon next to the address bar usually tell you?`,choices:[`You are logged in to your account on the site`,`The site is safe and run by a trusted company`,`The page has been checked for viruses and bugs`,`The connection to the site is encrypted`],answer:3,hint:`It is about how data travels, not about the site itself.`,explain:`The padlock means the connection uses HTTPS, so others on the network cannot read or change the data. It does not prove that the site itself is honest or safe.`},{id:`web-021`,level:1,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these status codes mean something went wrong? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`500 Internal Server Error`","`404 Not Found`","`200 OK`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`Read the words after each number.`,explain:"`404` and `500` are error codes: the page was missing, or the server failed. `200` means everything worked."},{id:`web-024`,level:1,topic:`HTTPS`,type:`fill`,prompt:`In HTTPS, what word does the final S stand for? Type one word.`,answer:[`Secure`],hint:`It is what the padlock icon is about.`,explain:`HTTPS means HTTP Secure. It is regular HTTP sent through an encrypted connection.`},{id:`web-025`,level:2,topic:`DNS`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does DNS do?`,choices:[`Encrypts the data sent between browser and server`,`Finds the IP address for a domain name`,`Checks your HTML for errors before it loads`,`Stores copies of pages so they load faster`],answer:1,hint:`Computers find each other by numbers, but people prefer names.`,explain:"DNS (Domain Name System) works like a phone book for the internet. It looks up the IP address for a name such as `example.com` so the browser can connect."},{id:`web-030`,level:2,topic:`URLs`,type:`single`,prompt:"In this URL, what is `3000`?",code:`http://localhost:3000`,lang:`text`,choices:[`The IP address`,`The port number`,`The page number`,`The file size`],answer:1,hint:`One computer can run many servers, each listening on its own number.`,explain:`The number after the colon is the port. It tells the browser which program on that computer to connect to.`},{id:`web-029`,level:2,topic:`URLs`,type:`single`,prompt:"In this URL, what is the part `?page=2&sort=new` called?",code:`https://example.com/products?page=2&sort=new`,lang:`text`,choices:[`The path`,`The fragment`,`The request header`,`The query string`],answer:3,hint:`It starts with a question mark and carries extra parameters.`,explain:"The query string starts with `?` and holds `key=value` pairs joined by `&`. Servers and scripts use it for things like page numbers and filters."},{id:`web-039`,level:2,topic:`URLs`,type:`single`,prompt:"In this URL, what does `#install` do?",code:`https://example.com/docs#install`,lang:`text`,choices:["Sends `install` to the server as a search term","Jumps to the element with the id `install`",`Tells the server which page section to send`,`Downloads and installs a program`],answer:1,hint:"The part after `#` is called the fragment.",explain:"The fragment after `#` tells the browser to scroll to the element with that id. It stays in the browser and is not sent to the server."},{id:`web-033`,level:2,topic:`DevTools`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does the Network panel in DevTools show you?`,choices:[`The speed and the name of your Wi-Fi connection`,`Each request the page sends, with its status`,`The CSS styles applied to each element`,`The HTML of the page as a tree of elements`],answer:1,hint:`It is about traffic between the page and servers.`,explain:`The Network panel lists every file and API request, with status codes, sizes, and timing. The HTML tree is in the Elements panel instead.`},{id:`web-041`,level:2,topic:`DOM`,type:`tf`,prompt:'`document.getElementById("menu")` returns `null` when no element has the id `menu`.',answer:!0,hint:`Missing elements do not cause an error. Think about which empty value comes back.`,explain:"True. When nothing matches, `getElementById` returns `null`. Check for `null` before you use the result, or you will get an error."},{id:`web-045`,level:2,topic:`Headers`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are real HTTP header names? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`Content-Type`","`Host`","`User-Agent`","`Page-Title`"],answer:[0,1,2],hint:`One of these sounds like part of the HTML, not the HTTP message.`,explain:"`Content-Type` describes the body, `Host` names the site, and `User-Agent` names the browser. The page title lives in the HTML `<title>` element, not in a header."},{id:`web-048`,level:2,topic:`DOM`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Complete the code so it selects the first `<p>` element on the page. Type only the method name.",code:`const p = document.____("p")`,lang:`js`,answer:[`querySelector`,`querySelector()`],hint:`It takes any CSS selector and returns one element.`,explain:'`document.querySelector("p")` returns the first paragraph on the page, or `null` if there is none.'},{id:`web-037`,level:2,topic:`HTTP Methods`,type:`single`,prompt:`Which HTTP method is commonly used to remove a resource, like deleting a blog post through an API?`,choices:["`REMOVE`","`GET`","`DROP`","`DELETE`"],answer:3,hint:`The method name says exactly what it does.`,explain:"`DELETE` asks the server to remove the resource at that URL. `REMOVE` and `DROP` are not HTTP methods."},{id:`web-026`,level:2,topic:`HTTP Methods`,type:`single`,prompt:`This request is sent to an API. What is it most likely trying to do?`,code:`PUT /api/users/7 HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Content-Type: application/json

{"name": "Ada"}`,lang:`http`,choices:[`Update user 7 with new data`,`Read the details of user 7`,`Create a brand new user called Ada`,`Delete user 7 from the database`],answer:0,hint:`Look at the method and the data in the body.`,explain:"`PUT` is commonly used to replace or update a resource that already exists. Here it sends new data for the user with id 7. Creating a new user is usually a `POST` to `/api/users`."},{id:`web-040`,level:2,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`tf`,prompt:"Status codes in the 200s (like `200` and `201`) mean the request succeeded.",answer:!0,hint:`Think of the most common happy code.`,explain:"True. All `2xx` codes mean success. For example, `201 Created` means a new resource was created."},{id:`web-027`,level:2,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does the status code `403 Forbidden` mean?",choices:[`You are not allowed to access this resource`,`The page does not exist on this server`,`The server crashed while handling the request`,`The page moved to a new address`],answer:0,hint:"Compare it with `401`, which is about not being logged in.",explain:"`403` means the server understood the request but will not let you access that resource. A missing page would be `404` instead."},{id:`web-028`,level:2,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`single`,prompt:"Status codes that start with `4` (like `400` or `404`) usually mean what?",choices:[`The request worked and data came back`,`The problem is on the client side`,`The problem is on the server side`,`The browser must go to a new URL`],answer:1,hint:"`5xx` codes are the server's fault. What about `4xx`?",explain:"`4xx` codes are client errors: the request was wrong, not allowed, or asked for something missing. `5xx` codes are server errors."},{id:`web-038`,level:2,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does the status code `301 Moved Permanently` tell the browser?",choices:[`The page now lives at a new URL`,`The page was deleted and is gone forever`,`The page is only moved for a short time`,`The user must log in before seeing the page`],answer:0,hint:"`3xx` codes are about redirection.",explain:"`301` is a permanent redirect. The response includes a `Location` header with the new URL, and the browser follows it. A short-term move uses `302` or `307` instead."},{id:`web-043`,level:2,topic:`HTTP Methods`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are real HTTP methods? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`PUT`","`FETCH`","`GET`","`PATCH`"],answer:[0,2,3],hint:"`fetch` is a JavaScript function, not a method in the HTTP spec.",explain:"`GET`, `PUT`, and `PATCH` are standard HTTP methods. `fetch()` is the browser function you use to send requests, not a method itself."},{id:`web-047`,level:2,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Which status code means "Internal Server Error"? Type only the three-digit number.`,answer:[`500`],hint:`Server errors start with 5.`,explain:"`500` means something broke on the server while handling the request. The fix is usually in the server code, not the browser."},{id:`web-031`,level:2,topic:`Headers`,type:`single`,prompt:`What are HTTP headers?`,choices:["The `<h1>` and `<h2>` titles at the top of a page",`Comments at the top of a JavaScript file`,"The first line of the HTML file, `<!DOCTYPE html>`",`Extra details sent with a request or response`],answer:3,hint:`They travel with the message, not inside the page content.`,explain:"Headers are `Name: value` lines that describe a request or response, such as its content type or caching rules. They are separate from HTML headings."},{id:`web-032`,level:2,topic:`Headers`,type:`single`,prompt:"A server sends this response. What does the `Content-Type` header tell the browser?",code:`HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"ok": true}`,lang:`http`,choices:[`The body is JSON data`,`The page is in English`,`The body is an image`,`The response is encrypted`],answer:0,hint:`Read the value of the header carefully.`,explain:"`Content-Type` describes the format of the body. `application/json` means the body is JSON, so code should parse it as JSON."},{id:`web-042`,level:2,topic:`DNS`,type:`tf`,prompt:`Your computer often caches DNS answers, so a second visit to the same site may skip the lookup.`,answer:!0,hint:`Would it make sense to ask the phone book for the same number again and again?`,explain:`True. DNS answers are saved for a while by the browser, the operating system, and your network. This makes repeat visits faster.`},{id:`web-036`,level:2,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:'The page has `<nav id="menu">`. Which line selects that element?',choices:['`document.getElementById("nav")`','`document.getElementById(".menu")`','`document.getElementById("menu")`','`document.getElementById("#menu")`'],answer:2,hint:`This method already knows it is looking for an id.`,explain:'`getElementById` takes the plain id value, without a `#`. The `#` is only needed in CSS selectors like `querySelector("#menu")`.'},{id:`web-034`,level:2,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does this code return?`,code:`document.querySelector(".title")`,lang:`js`,choices:["The first element with the class `title`","A new element with the class `title`","The page's `<title>` tag text","A list of every element with the class `title`"],answer:0,hint:`The dot in the selector works just like in CSS.`,explain:"`querySelector` takes a CSS selector and returns the first matching element, or `null` if none match. `.title` matches the class `title`."},{id:`web-035`,level:2,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does this code do to the heading?`,code:`const h = document.querySelector("h1")
h.textContent = "Welcome!"`,lang:`js`,choices:[`Adds "Welcome!" after the existing text`,"Creates a new `<h1>` element",`Logs "Welcome!" to the console`,`Replaces its text with "Welcome!"`],answer:3,hint:`You are setting a property on an element that already exists.`,explain:'Setting `textContent` replaces all the text inside the element, so the old text is gone. The first `<h1>` on the page now shows "Welcome!".'},{id:`web-044`,level:2,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these status codes mean the problem is on the server side? Select all that apply.`,choices:["`502`","`503`","`404`","`400`"],answer:[0,1],hint:`Look at the first digit only.`,explain:"`5xx` codes are server errors, for example `502 Bad Gateway` and `503 Service Unavailable`. `400` and `404` start with 4, so they are client errors."},{id:`web-046`,level:2,topic:`HTTP Methods`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Which HTTP method is usually used to send new data to a server, for example when submitting a sign-up form? Type the method name in capital letters.`,answer:[`POST`],hint:"`GET` reads data. You want the common method that sends a body to the server.",explain:"`POST` sends data in the request body so the server can create something new or process it."},{id:`web-049`,level:3,topic:`classList`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does this code do?`,code:`card.classList.add("active")`,lang:`js`,choices:["Removes the class `active`","Creates a new element called `active`","Adds `active` and keeps the other classes","Replaces all the element's classes with `active`"],answer:2,hint:"`classList` treats classes as a list you can add to.",explain:"`classList.add` adds a class without touching the classes already on the element. To replace all classes at once, you would set `className` instead."},{id:`web-050`,level:3,topic:`classList`,type:`single`,prompt:'What does `menu.classList.toggle("open")` do?',choices:["Checks if `open` exists and returns `true` or `false`","Adds `open` if missing, or removes it if present","Always adds `open`","Always removes `open`"],answer:1,hint:`Think of a light switch.`,explain:"`toggle` flips the class on or off. It is handy for opening and closing a menu with one button. Checking for a class without changing it is a different method."},{id:`web-061`,level:3,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does `popup.remove()` do?",choices:[`Hides the element but keeps its space`,`Takes the element out of the page`,`Deletes the HTML file from the server`,`Removes all CSS from the element`],answer:1,hint:`The name says it all.`,explain:"`remove()` takes the element out of the DOM, so it disappears from the page. Hiding with CSS would keep it in the DOM."},{id:`web-065`,level:3,topic:`localStorage`,type:`tf`,prompt:"Data saved with `localStorage` on one website can be read by every other website.",answer:!1,hint:`Would it be safe if any site could read your data from other sites?`,explain:"False. `localStorage` is separate for each origin (protocol, domain, and port). One site cannot read another site's storage."},{id:`web-051`,level:3,topic:`Creating Elements`,type:`single`,prompt:"After this line runs, where is the new `<li>`?",code:`const li = document.createElement("li")`,lang:`js`,choices:["It appears at the end of the `<body>` right away",`It exists in memory but is not on the page yet`,"It replaces the first `<li>` on the page",`It appears at the top of every list`],answer:1,hint:`Creating something and placing it are two separate steps.`,explain:"`createElement` only builds the element. You still need to insert it into the page before it shows up."},{id:`web-052`,level:3,topic:`Creating Elements`,type:`single`,prompt:'You created `li` with `document.createElement("li")`. Which line puts it at the end of the list `ul`?',choices:["`ul.appendChild(li)`","`li.show(ul)`","`ul.insert(li)`","`ul.createChild(li)`"],answer:0,hint:`You add a child to the parent.`,explain:"`appendChild` (or the newer `append`) inserts the element as the last child of the parent. Now it appears on the page."},{id:`web-072`,level:3,topic:`classList`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Which `classList` method checks whether an element has a class and returns `true` or `false`? Type only the method name.",answer:[`contains`,`contains()`],hint:`It is a common English verb for "has inside it".`,explain:'`el.classList.contains("open")` returns `true` if the element has the class `open`, and `false` if not.'},{id:`web-069`,level:3,topic:`DevTools`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these can browser DevTools help you do while debugging JavaScript? Select all that apply.`,choices:[`Read error messages in the Console`,`Pause code with a breakpoint in the Sources panel`,`Permanently change the live website for all visitors`],answer:[0,1],hint:`DevTools works on your copy of the page, in your own browser.`,explain:`You can read errors in the Console and set breakpoints to pause and inspect code. Edits in DevTools only change your local copy, and they are gone after a reload.`},{id:`web-053`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does this code do?`,code:`button.addEventListener("click", sayHi)`,lang:`js`,choices:["Runs `sayHi` once now and then on every click","Runs `sayHi` every time the button is clicked","Runs `sayHi` only on the first click, then stops",`Clicks the button automatically`],answer:1,hint:`You are giving the browser a function to call later.`,explain:"`addEventListener` registers `sayHi` as a listener. The browser does not call it now; it calls it each time a `click` event happens on the button."},{id:`web-064`,level:3,type:`tf`,topic:`Events`,prompt:"In this `submit` listener, `event.preventDefault()` stops the browser from reloading the page.",code:`form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault()
  showMessage("Thanks!")
})`,lang:`js`,answer:!0,hint:`What does a form do by default when you submit it?`,explain:"By default, submitting a form sends it and loads a new page. `preventDefault()` cancels that default action, so your JavaScript can handle the data instead."},{id:`web-054`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why does `sayHi` run as soon as the page loads, instead of on click?",code:`button.addEventListener("click", sayHi())`,lang:`js`,choices:["`sayHi()` calls the function right away",`Click events always fire once on page load`,`Buttons cannot have click listeners`,'The event name should be `"onclick"`'],answer:0,hint:"Compare `sayHi` with `sayHi()`.",explain:'Adding `()` calls the function immediately. To run it on click, pass the function itself: `addEventListener("click", sayHi)`.'},{id:`web-056`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`single`,prompt:"Inside an event listener, what is `event.target`?",choices:[`The function that handles the event`,'The type of the event, like `"click"`',`The URL of the current page`,`The element where the event happened`],answer:3,hint:`If you click a button, which element was hit?`,explain:"`event.target` is the element that started the event, such as the exact button that was clicked. The event name is in `event.type` instead."},{id:`web-057`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`single`,prompt:"Which event fires every time the value of a text `<input>` changes while the user types?",choices:["`submit`","`input`","`change`","`load`"],answer:1,hint:`One of these waits until the user leaves the field. You want the one that fires on every keystroke.`,explain:"The `input` event fires on each change to the value, so it is great for live search. The `change` event on a text field waits until the user leaves the field."},{id:`web-055`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`single`,prompt:"Inside a `keydown` listener, which property tells you which key the user pressed?",choices:["`event.key`","`event.type`","`event.value`","`event.button`"],answer:0,hint:`Think about the thing on the keyboard you press, not the type of event.`,explain:'`event.key` holds the pressed key, such as `"a"`, `"Enter"`, or `"Escape"`. `event.type` holds the event name, here `"keydown"`.'},{id:`web-071`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Complete the code so `sayHi` no longer runs when the button is clicked. Type only the method name.",code:`button.____("click", sayHi)`,lang:`js`,answer:[`removeEventListener`,`removeEventListener()`],hint:"Every `add...` method in the DOM usually has a partner that undoes it.",explain:"`removeEventListener` takes the same event name and the same function you added. After it runs, clicks no longer call `sayHi`."},{id:`web-068`,level:3,topic:`classList`,type:`multi`,prompt:'The element starts as `<div class="card">`. Which classes does it have after this code runs? Select all that apply.',code:`el.classList.add("active")
el.classList.toggle("card")
el.classList.add("big")`,lang:`js`,choices:["`active`","`card`","`big`"],answer:[0,2],hint:"Go line by line, and think about what `toggle` does to a class that is already there.",explain:'`add` puts `active` and `big` on the element. `toggle("card")` removes `card` because it was already there. The result is `class="active big"`.'},{id:`web-062`,level:3,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does this code give you?`,code:`const items = document.querySelectorAll(".item")`,lang:`js`,choices:[`A string of HTML`,"Only the first element that has the class `item`","The number of `.item` elements","A NodeList of every element with class `item`"],answer:3,hint:`Notice the word "All".`,explain:"`querySelectorAll` returns a NodeList of all matches. You can loop over it with `forEach` or `for...of`."},{id:`web-063`,level:3,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:'The page has `<input id="name">`. How do you read what the user typed into it?',code:`const input = document.getElementById("name")`,lang:`js`,choices:["`input.textContent`","`input.typed`","`input.innerHTML`","`input.value`"],answer:3,hint:`Form fields keep their current content in a special property.`,explain:"Form fields store their current content in `value`. An `<input>` has no text children, so `textContent` is empty."},{id:`web-058`,level:3,topic:`DOM`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does the page show after each line runs?`,code:`a.innerHTML = "<b>Hi</b>"
b.textContent = "<b>Hi</b>"`,lang:`js`,choices:[`Both show bold "Hi"`,'`a` shows bold "Hi", `b` shows the literal text `<b>Hi</b>`','`a` shows the literal text, `b` shows bold "Hi"',"Both show the literal text `<b>Hi</b>`"],answer:1,hint:`One property reads the string as HTML, the other as plain text.`,explain:"`innerHTML` parses the string as HTML, so the `<b>` tag makes bold text. `textContent` treats everything as plain text and shows the tags as characters."},{id:`web-059`,level:3,topic:`localStorage`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does this code do?`,code:`localStorage.setItem("theme", "dark")`,lang:`js`,choices:[`Saves the value only until the tab is closed`,`Switches the page to dark mode right away`,'Saves `"dark"` under `"theme"`, even after a restart','Saves `"theme"` in a cookie that is sent to the server'],answer:2,hint:`It stores a value; it does not change any styles by itself.`,explain:"`localStorage` keeps key/value pairs in the browser for that site, and the data stays after the browser restarts. It does not change any styles or talk to the server by itself."},{id:`web-066`,level:3,topic:`localStorage`,type:`tf`,prompt:'`localStorage` stores values as strings, so saving the number `5` gives back `"5"`.',answer:!0,hint:"Try `typeof localStorage.getItem(...)` in the Console.",explain:"True. Values are converted to strings. To save objects, use `JSON.stringify` when saving and `JSON.parse` when reading."},{id:`web-060`,level:3,topic:`localStorage`,type:`single`,prompt:'No value was ever saved under the key `"score"`. What does this return?',code:`localStorage.getItem("score")`,lang:`js`,choices:["`undefined`","`0`","`null`",'An empty string `""`'],answer:2,hint:"It is the same value `querySelector` gives when nothing matches.",explain:"`getItem` returns `null` when the key does not exist. You can check for `null` and use a default value instead."},{id:`web-070`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`fill`,prompt:"What event name do you pass to `addEventListener` to react when a button is clicked? Type the event name exactly as in code.",answer:[`click`,`"click"`],hint:`It is a single lowercase word.`,explain:'The event is called `click`: `button.addEventListener("click", handler)`.'},{id:`web-067`,level:3,topic:`Events`,type:`multi`,prompt:"Which of these are real DOM event names you can pass to `addEventListener`? Select all that apply.",choices:["`tap`","`keydown`","`hover`","`submit`"],answer:[1,3],hint:"CSS has `:hover`, but the DOM event for the mouse entering an element has a different name.",explain:"`keydown` and `submit` are standard events. There is no `hover` or `tap` event; for hovering you can use `mouseenter` or `pointerover`."},{id:`web-073`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does `fetch(url)` return right away?",choices:[`A Promise that resolves to a Response`,`The parsed JSON data from the server`,`The page's HTML as a DOM element`,`The response text, once it has loaded`],answer:0,hint:`Network requests take time, so the result is not ready instantly.`,explain:"`fetch` returns a Promise. When the server answers, it resolves to a `Response` object that you can read with methods like `.json()`."},{id:`web-089`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`tf`,prompt:"`fetch` is built into modern browsers, so you do not need to install a package to use it.",answer:!0,hint:"Try typing `fetch` in the Console of any page.",explain:"True. `fetch` is a standard browser API, so it is ready to use in every modern browser."},{id:`web-074`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does `data` hold after this code runs?",code:`const response = await fetch("/api/user")
const data = await response.json()`,lang:`js`,choices:[`The response headers as an object`,`The raw JSON text of the body, as a string`,`The body, parsed into a JavaScript value`,`The HTTP status code`],answer:2,hint:"The `.json()` method does some work for you.",explain:"`response.json()` reads the body and parses it as JSON. You get a normal JavaScript object or array back, not a string."},{id:`web-077`,level:4,topic:`JSON`,type:`single`,prompt:"Why is `JSON.stringify` used in this request?",code:`fetch("/api/notes", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ text: "Buy milk" })
})`,lang:`js`,choices:[`To encrypt the data before sending it`,`To read the JSON the server sends back`,`To make the request smaller and faster`,`To turn the object into a JSON string`],answer:3,hint:`An HTTP body is sent as text, not as a live JavaScript object.`,explain:"A request body must be text or binary data. `JSON.stringify` turns the object into a JSON string, and the header tells the server it is JSON."},{id:`web-075`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`single`,prompt:"The server answers a `fetch` request with `404 Not Found`. What happens?",choices:[`The browser shows its own 404 page`,"The `fetch` call retries until it gets `200 OK`","It resolves, but `response.ok` is `false`","The Promise rejects, so the `catch` block runs"],answer:2,hint:`From the network's point of view, the server did reply.`,explain:"`fetch` only rejects on network failures, like being offline. For HTTP errors you need to check `response.ok` or `response.status` yourself."},{id:`web-076`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`single`,prompt:"When is `response.ok` equal to `true`?",choices:[`When the response body is valid JSON`,`When the status code is from 200 to 299`,`When the server sent a non-empty body`,`When the request used a secure HTTPS URL`],answer:1,hint:`It is about the success group of status codes.`,explain:"`response.ok` is `true` for any `2xx` status. It is a quick way to check success before reading the body."},{id:`web-094`,level:4,topic:`npm`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Which folder does `npm install` put downloaded packages into? Type the folder name.",answer:[`node_modules`,`node_modules/`,`./node_modules`],hint:`It is named after Node and the modules it holds.`,explain:"Packages go into `node_modules`. This folder is usually not committed to Git, because `npm install` can rebuild it."},{id:`web-091`,level:4,topic:`JSON`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are valid JSON? Select all that apply.`,choices:['`{"name": "Ada"}`','`{"age": 30,}`','`{name: "Ada"}`',"`[1, 2, 3]`"],answer:[0,3],hint:`Watch out for missing quotes and extra commas.`,explain:'JSON needs quoted keys and does not allow trailing commas. So `{name: "Ada"}` and `{"age": 30,}` are invalid.'},{id:`web-088`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`tf`,prompt:"When you call `fetch(url)` with no options, it sends a `GET` request.",answer:!0,hint:"Think about the most common reason to call `fetch`: reading data.",explain:'True. `GET` is the default method. To send data, pass an options object with a `method` such as `"POST"` and a `body`.'},{id:`web-078`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`single`,prompt:"Your page is open at `https://myapp.com/home`. Which URL does this code request?",code:`fetch("/api/todos")`,lang:`js`,choices:["`https://myapp.com/api/todos`","`https://myapp.com/home/api/todos`","`https://api.todos`","`http://localhost/api/todos`"],answer:0,hint:"A path that starts with `/` begins at the root of the current site.",explain:"A URL that starts with `/` is resolved against the current origin, so it becomes `https://myapp.com/api/todos`. Without the leading `/`, it would be relative to the current folder instead."},{id:`web-081`,level:4,topic:`Storage`,type:`single`,prompt:"How is `sessionStorage` different from `localStorage`?",choices:["`sessionStorage` is sent to the server automatically","`sessionStorage` is cleared when the tab is closed","`sessionStorage` can store objects without converting them","`sessionStorage` is shared between all websites"],answer:1,hint:`The name hints at how long the data lives.`,explain:"`sessionStorage` works like `localStorage`, but its data belongs to one tab and is cleared when that tab closes. `localStorage` stays until it is removed."},{id:`web-082`,level:4,topic:`Cookies`,type:`single`,prompt:"What is a key difference between cookies and `localStorage`?",choices:["Cookies can hold much more data than `localStorage`",`Cookies can only be created by JavaScript`,`Cookies are deleted every time you close a tab`,`Cookies are sent to the server with requests`],answer:3,hint:`Think about which one the server can see without extra code.`,explain:"The browser sends cookies to the server in the `Cookie` header on matching requests, which makes them useful for login sessions. `localStorage` stays in the browser unless your code sends it."},{id:`web-079`,level:4,topic:`CORS`,type:`single`,prompt:`What is CORS, in simple terms?`,choices:[`A server setting that makes images and files load faster`,`A JavaScript library that helps you build forms`,`A type of cookie that is shared between websites`,`A browser rule about reading data from other origins`],answer:3,hint:`It is about one site's page reading data from a different site.`,explain:"Browsers block a page from reading responses from other origins unless that server allows it with headers like `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. That system is CORS."},{id:`web-080`,level:4,topic:`CORS`,type:`single`,prompt:"Which URL has the same origin as `https://shop.example.com/cart`?",choices:["`https://shop.example.com:8443/cart`","`https://example.com/cart`","`https://shop.example.com/checkout`","`http://shop.example.com/cart`"],answer:2,hint:`An origin is the protocol, the host, and the port together. The path does not count.`,explain:"Only the path is different in `https://shop.example.com/checkout`, so it has the same origin. Changing the protocol, host, or port makes a different origin."},{id:`web-095`,level:4,topic:`Fetch`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Complete the code so it logs the numeric status code, like `404`. Type only the property name.",code:`const response = await fetch("/api/posts")
console.log(response.____)`,lang:`js`,answer:[`status`],hint:"`response.ok` is only `true` or `false`. You want the number instead.",explain:"`response.status` holds the number, such as `200` or `404`. `response.ok` is `true` only when this number is from 200 to 299."},{id:`web-093`,level:4,topic:`Script Loading`,type:`multi`,prompt:"Which of these let the browser show the HTML content without waiting for `app.js` to download? Select all that apply.",choices:['`<script src="app.js" defer>` in the `<head>`','`<script src="app.js">` in the `<head>`','`<script src="app.js">` at the end of the `<body>`'],answer:[0,2],hint:`Does the parser have to stop at the script before it reaches the content?`,explain:"A plain script in the `<head>` stops parsing until it downloads and runs, so the page stays blank. With `defer`, or with the script after the content, the HTML is parsed first."},{id:`web-086`,level:4,topic:`npm`,type:`single`,prompt:`What is npm mainly used for?`,choices:[`Running your JavaScript code in the browser tab`,`Hosting your website so others can visit it`,`Checking your HTML and CSS for mistakes`,`Installing and managing JavaScript packages`],answer:3,hint:"Think about what you type `npm install` for.",explain:`npm is a package manager. It downloads packages that other people published and keeps track of them in your project. It does not host your site or check your code.`},{id:`web-087`,level:4,topic:`npm`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does this command do in a project folder?`,code:`npm uninstall dayjs`,lang:`bash`,choices:["Removes `dayjs` from `node_modules` and `package.json`","Removes `dayjs` from the npm website for everyone","Marks `dayjs` as old but keeps it installed","Removes `dayjs` only from `package.json`, not the disk"],answer:0,hint:"It is the opposite of `npm install dayjs`.",explain:"`npm uninstall` deletes the package files and removes it from your dependencies list. It only changes your project, never the package on the npm website."},{id:`web-083`,level:4,topic:`Script Loading`,type:`single`,prompt:"This script is in the `<head>`. Why does it log `null`?",code:`<head>
  <script>
    console.log(document.querySelector("#app"))
  <\/script>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="app"></div>
</body>`,lang:`html`,choices:["The script runs before the `<div>` is parsed","`querySelector` cannot find elements by id","Scripts in the `<head>` cannot use `document`","The `<div>` is empty, so it does not count"],answer:0,hint:`The browser reads HTML from top to bottom.`,explain:"The browser runs a plain script as soon as it reaches it, and the `<div>` comes later in the file. Load the script with `defer`, or move it to the end of `<body>`."},{id:`web-084`,level:4,topic:`Script Loading`,type:`single`,prompt:"What is true about a script with the `async` attribute?",code:`<script src="analytics.js" async><\/script>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`It waits and runs after all the other scripts finish`,`It stops HTML parsing until it has downloaded`,`It runs as soon as it downloads, in any order`,`It turns all its functions into async functions`],answer:2,hint:`Async means it does not wait for anything else.`,explain:"An `async` script downloads in parallel and runs as soon as it is ready. That is fine for independent scripts like analytics, but not for scripts that depend on each other."},{id:`web-085`,level:4,topic:`Page Events`,type:`single`,prompt:"When does the `DOMContentLoaded` event fire?",choices:[`When the user first clicks on the page`,`As soon as the first byte of HTML arrives`,`When the HTML is parsed and the DOM is ready`,`Only after every image has finished loading`],answer:2,hint:`Its name mentions the DOM, not images.`,explain:"`DOMContentLoaded` fires once the DOM is ready, without waiting for images to load. It is a safe time to start working with elements."},{id:`web-090`,level:4,topic:`CORS`,type:`tf`,prompt:"The browser enforces CORS, and a server allows other origins to read its responses by sending headers like `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.",answer:!0,hint:`Who sends the permission, and who checks it?`,explain:`True. The server gives permission with CORS headers, and the browser checks them. That is why CORS errors are usually fixed on the server.`},{id:`web-092`,level:4,topic:`Storage`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these can still hold your data after you close and reopen the browser? Select all that apply.`,choices:[`A normal JavaScript variable`,`A cookie with an expiry date`,"`localStorage`","`sessionStorage`"],answer:[1,2],hint:`Some storage only lives as long as a tab, or as long as the page is open.`,explain:"`localStorage` and cookies with an expiry date are kept on disk. `sessionStorage` is cleared when the tab closes, and variables are lost when the page unloads."},{id:`web-096`,level:4,topic:`npm`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Which command creates a new `package.json` file in an empty project folder? Complete it: `npm ____`. Type only the missing word.",answer:[`init`,`init -y`,`init --yes`],hint:`It is the first command you run when you start a new project.`,explain:"`npm init` asks a few questions and then writes `package.json`. Add `-y` to accept all the default answers."},{id:`web-097`,level:5,topic:`Event Bubbling`,type:`single`,prompt:"The user clicks the `<li>`. What happens with the listener on the `<ul>`?",code:`<ul id="list">
  <li>Apples</li>
</ul>
<script>
  document.getElementById("list").addEventListener("click", () => {
    console.log("list clicked")
  })
<\/script>`,lang:`html`,choices:[`It throws an error`,`It runs twice`,`It runs, because the click bubbles up`,"It does not run, because the `<li>` was clicked, not the `<ul>`"],answer:2,hint:`Events travel from the clicked element up through its parents.`,explain:"Most events bubble: after reaching the `<li>`, the click moves up to the `<ul>`, then the body, and so on. So the `<ul>` listener runs."},{id:`web-098`,level:5,topic:`Event Bubbling`,type:`single`,prompt:"What is a main benefit of event delegation (one listener on a parent that checks `event.target`)?",choices:["It removes the need to call `addEventListener`",`It makes events stop bubbling`,`It makes clicks register twice as fast`,`It also works for child elements added later`],answer:3,hint:`Think of a list where items are added after the page loads.`,explain:`With delegation, the parent listener catches events that bubble up from any child, including new ones. You need fewer listeners and no re-wiring.`},{id:`web-099`,level:5,topic:`Event Bubbling`,type:`single`,prompt:"The listener is on the `<ul>`, and the user clicks one of its `<li>` items. What is `event.currentTarget`?",code:`ul.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  console.log(event.target, event.currentTarget)
})`,lang:`js`,choices:["The `document`","The `window`","The clicked `<li>`","The `<ul>`"],answer:3,hint:`One property is where the event started, the other is where it is being handled now.`,explain:"`event.target` is the clicked `<li>`, while `event.currentTarget` is the element whose listener is running, here the `<ul>`."},{id:`web-100`,level:5,topic:`Page Events`,type:`single`,prompt:"How is the `window` `load` event different from `DOMContentLoaded`?",choices:["`load` fires first, before the HTML is parsed","`load` fires every time the user scrolls","`load` only fires for pages without scripts","`load` also waits for images and stylesheets"],answer:3,hint:`Which one waits for everything?`,explain:"`DOMContentLoaded` fires when the DOM is ready. `load` fires later, after images, stylesheets, and other resources have also finished."},{id:`web-114`,level:5,topic:`Cookies`,type:`tf`,prompt:"A cookie with the `HttpOnly` flag cannot be read by JavaScript through `document.cookie`.",answer:!0,hint:`Think about what the flag hides the cookie from.`,explain:"True. `HttpOnly` cookies are still sent to the server, but scripts cannot read them. This protects session cookies if an XSS bug happens."},{id:`web-110`,level:5,topic:`npm`,type:`single`,prompt:"In `package.json`, what does the `~` in this version allow?",code:`{
  "dependencies": {
    "dayjs": "~1.11.0"
  }
}`,lang:`json`,choices:["Updates like `1.12.0`, but not `2.0.0`","Updates like `1.11.5`, but not `1.12.0`","Only exactly `1.11.0`, never any update","Any version close to `1.11.0`, up or down"],answer:1,hint:"The tilde `~` is stricter than the caret `^`.",explain:"`~1.11.0` allows only patch updates, so any `1.11.x` from `1.11.0` up. The caret `^1.11.0` would also allow minor updates like `1.12.0`."},{id:`web-120`,level:5,topic:`Event Bubbling`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Which `event` method stops an event from bubbling up to parent elements? Type only the method name.",answer:[`stopPropagation`,`stopPropagation()`],hint:"It is called on the `event` object inside the listener of the child element.",explain:"`event.stopPropagation()` stops the event at the current element, so listeners on parent elements do not receive it."},{id:`web-117`,level:5,topic:`Security`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these are good security habits? Select all that apply.`,choices:[`Trust hidden form fields because users cannot see them`,`Put the database password in a frontend config file`,"Use `textContent` for text that users wrote",`Validate all input on the server`],answer:[2,3],hint:`Never trust anything that comes from the browser.`,explain:`Server-side validation and safe text rendering protect your app. Frontend files and hidden fields can be read and changed by anyone.`},{id:`web-102`,level:5,topic:`Performance`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does minifying a JavaScript file do?`,choices:[`Hides the code so that users can no longer read it`,`Makes the file smaller by removing spaces and comments`,`Splits the file into many small files for the browser`,`Converts the code into faster machine code`],answer:1,hint:`Think about what the user has to download.`,explain:`A minifier removes spaces and comments and shortens names, so the file downloads faster. The code works the same, and anyone can still read it in DevTools.`},{id:`web-101`,level:5,topic:`Performance`,type:`single`,prompt:`A photo is 4000 pixels wide but is shown only 400 pixels wide on the page. What is the best fix?`,choices:[`Serve a smaller, compressed copy of the image`,"Set a CSS `width` of 400px and keep the big file",`Load the image twice so the browser caches it`,`Convert the photo to a GIF so it loads faster`],answer:0,hint:`CSS can shrink how it looks, but not how much the user downloads.`,explain:`The browser still downloads the full file even when CSS shrinks it. A file about 400 pixels wide, well compressed, saves data and loads faster. GIF files are usually larger for photos.`},{id:`web-103`,level:5,topic:`Caching`,type:`single`,prompt:"A server sends this header with `logo.png`. What does it allow?",code:`Cache-Control: max-age=3600`,lang:`http`,choices:[`The browser can reuse its copy for one hour`,`The server deletes the image after one hour`,`The image can only be downloaded 3600 times`,`The server waits one hour before sending it`],answer:0,hint:"`max-age` is measured in seconds.",explain:"`max-age=3600` means the cached copy stays fresh for 3600 seconds (one hour). During that time the browser can skip the network request."},{id:`web-104`,level:5,topic:`Security`,type:`single`,prompt:`This code shows a comment that a user wrote. What is the risk?`,code:`commentBox.innerHTML = userComment`,lang:`js`,choices:[`There is no risk, because a comment is only text`,`The HTML in the comment will show as plain characters`,`A comment with HTML tags could run scripts (XSS)`,`Very long comments will crash the server`],answer:2,hint:`What if the comment contains HTML instead of plain words?`,explain:"`innerHTML` parses user text as HTML, so attackers can inject markup that runs scripts. This is cross-site scripting (XSS). Use `textContent` for user text."},{id:`web-112`,level:5,topic:`Security`,type:`tf`,prompt:`Using HTTPS also protects your site from XSS attacks.`,answer:!1,hint:`HTTPS protects data on the way. Where does XSS code run?`,explain:`False. HTTPS encrypts data between the browser and the server. XSS happens when your own page runs injected code, so you still need to handle user input safely.`},{id:`web-105`,level:5,topic:`Security`,type:`single`,prompt:`Your app needs a secret API key to call a paid service. Where should the key live?`,choices:[`In an HTML comment so users cannot see it`,"In `localStorage` so it is saved safely",`In a JavaScript variable in the frontend code`,`On your server, never in browser code`],answer:3,hint:`Anyone can read everything the browser downloads.`,explain:`Frontend code is visible to every visitor through DevTools, even in comments or storage. Keep secrets on the server, and let the frontend call your server instead.`},{id:`web-119`,level:5,topic:`Status Codes`,type:`fill`,prompt:`Which status code means "Unauthorized", for example when you must log in first? Type only the three-digit number.`,answer:[`401`],hint:"It is a 4xx code that sits close to `403 Forbidden`.",explain:"`401 Unauthorized` means the request needs valid login details. `403 Forbidden` means you are known but still not allowed."},{id:`web-115`,level:5,topic:`Accessibility`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these help people who use only a keyboard? Select all that apply.`,choices:["Using real `<button>` elements for actions",`Keeping a logical Tab order`,"Removing `outline` from focused elements with no replacement",`Keeping a visible focus outline`],answer:[0,1,3],hint:`Keyboard users need to see where they are and reach every control.`,explain:`A visible focus style shows where the user is, buttons work with the keyboard by default, and a logical order makes Tab predictable. Removing the outline hides the focus.`},{id:`web-109`,level:5,topic:`DevTools`,type:`single`,prompt:`What does the Lighthouse panel in Chrome DevTools do?`,choices:[`Makes your page load faster for all visitors`,`Checks a page for speed, accessibility, and more`,`Shows the requests your page made, one by one`,`Finds and fixes bugs in your JavaScript for you`],answer:1,hint:`It is like a report card for your page.`,explain:`Lighthouse runs audits and gives scores for performance, accessibility, and best practices, with tips. It only reports problems; you still make the fixes yourself.`},{id:`web-107`,level:5,topic:`Accessibility`,type:`single`,prompt:`A form marks wrong fields only by turning their border red. Why is this a problem?`,choices:[`Red borders do not show up in some browsers`,`Some users cannot see the color change`,`Screen readers read the color out loud too often`,`Accessibility rules do not allow red on forms`],answer:1,hint:`Not everyone sees colors the same way.`,explain:`Many people have some color blindness, so a red border alone is easy to miss. Add a short text message too, so everyone, including screen reader users, knows what went wrong.`},{id:`web-113`,level:5,topic:`Accessibility`,type:`tf`,prompt:`Low color contrast between text and its background makes the text harder to read for many people.`,answer:!0,hint:`Think of light gray text on a white background.`,explain:`True. Good contrast helps people with low vision and anyone reading in bright sunlight. WCAG suggests at least a 4.5:1 ratio for normal text.`},{id:`web-108`,level:5,topic:`Accessibility`,type:`single`,prompt:`What is a quick way to check that keyboard users can use your page?`,choices:[`Zoom the page to 200% and check the layout`,`Unplug the mouse and use Tab, Enter, and Space`,`Look at the page on a phone with a small screen`,`Run the page through an HTML validator`],answer:1,hint:`Try being a keyboard user yourself.`,explain:`Move through the page with Tab and Shift+Tab, and press Enter or Space on controls. You should always see where the focus is and be able to reach every button and link.`},{id:`web-106`,level:5,topic:`Security`,type:`single`,prompt:`Your sign-up form already checks the email format in JavaScript. Should the server check it too?`,choices:[`Only if the site uses HTTP instead of HTTPS`,`Yes, users can skip or change frontend checks`,`No, checking twice makes the form too slow`,`No, the JavaScript check already blocks bad emails`],answer:1,hint:`Can someone send a request without using your form at all?`,explain:`Anyone can send requests directly or edit your JavaScript in DevTools. Frontend checks help users, but the server must validate every input.`},{id:`web-111`,level:5,topic:`npm`,type:`single`,prompt:"What does `npm audit` do?",choices:[`Checks your packages for known security problems`,`Finds bugs and typos in your own JavaScript code`,`Shows how much disk space each package uses`,`Uploads your code to npm for a security review`],answer:0,hint:`It looks at your dependencies, not at the code you wrote.`,explain:"`npm audit` compares your installed packages with a database of known security problems and suggests fixes. It does not check the code you wrote yourself."},{id:`web-118`,level:5,topic:`npm`,type:`fill`,prompt:"Complete the command that updates installed packages to the newest versions that `package.json` allows: `npm ____`. Type only the missing word.",answer:[`update`,`up`,`upgrade`],hint:"It is not `install`. It is the word for refreshing what you already have.",explain:"`npm update` installs newer versions that still match the version ranges in `package.json`, such as `^` or `~`. It also updates `package-lock.json`."},{id:`web-116`,level:5,topic:`Performance`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which of these usually make a page load faster? Select all that apply.`,choices:[`Letting the browser cache static files`,`Auto-playing a large uncompressed video at page start`,`Compressing images`,"Using `defer` on scripts that are not needed right away"],answer:[0,2,3],hint:`Faster means less to download and less blocking.`,explain:`Smaller images, cached files, and deferred scripts all reduce download or blocking time. A huge video at start does the opposite.`}]};export{e as default};